1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:21,260
This is Doing and Feeling, Episode 11, Theories on Trial. In 1998, the neuroscientist Christoph Koch made a bet with the philosopher David Chalmers. Koch wagered a case of fine wine that within 25 years, science would clearly identify the brain mechanism behind consciousness. Chalmers bet against.

2
00:00:21,260 --> 00:00:24,140
25 years later, in June 2023,

3
00:00:24,700 --> 00:00:30,000
at the annual meeting of the Association for the Scientific Study of Consciousness in New York,

4
00:00:30,520 --> 00:00:33,920
Koch conceded on stage and handed over a case of Madera.

5
00:00:34,540 --> 00:00:38,820
Then the two of them renewed the bet, with a new deadline of 2048.

6
00:00:39,120 --> 00:00:41,480
The concession came at the same session,

7
00:00:41,660 --> 00:00:46,620
where the first results were presented from an experiment pitting two leading theories against each other,

8
00:00:46,820 --> 00:00:50,680
with their own champions agreeing in advance on what would count as failure.

9
00:00:51,260 --> 00:00:52,760
We'll end with that experiment.

10
00:00:53,240 --> 00:00:57,040
First, the theories and a problem with how they've been tested.

11
00:00:58,080 --> 00:01:00,520
What should a theory of consciousness explain?

12
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:03,580
Why some contents are conscious and others are not?

13
00:01:03,800 --> 00:01:06,480
Why we're conscious at some times and not others?

14
00:01:06,780 --> 00:01:09,920
Why experiences have the specific character they do?

15
00:01:10,300 --> 00:01:12,000
Why consciousness is unified?

16
00:01:12,320 --> 00:01:16,080
And what it adds to an organism that processes information anyway?

17
00:01:16,540 --> 00:01:18,540
No theory explains all five.

18
00:01:18,540 --> 00:01:26,440
A 2022 review by Anil Seth and Tim Bain listed 22 theories in circulation and examined four

19
00:01:26,440 --> 00:01:32,780
families in depth. Seth has also argued for a strategy he calls addressing the real problem.

20
00:01:32,960 --> 00:01:38,660
Rather than solving or dissolving the hard problem head-on, explain, predict, and control

21
00:01:38,660 --> 00:01:44,260
the properties of experience, the way biology dissolved the mystery of life, not by finding

22
00:01:44,260 --> 00:01:50,100
a vital spark, but by explaining metabolism, reproduction, and heredity one at a time.

23
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:55,720
Whether consciousness will yield the same way is exactly what the hard problem of Episode 7

24
00:01:55,720 --> 00:02:01,280
disputes. But the strategy has made the field productive. Where do researchers themselves stand?

25
00:02:01,500 --> 00:02:09,000
A survey of 166 attendees of the field's main conference in 2018 and 2019, published in 2022,

26
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:15,480
gives a snapshot. Predictive processing was rated promising by about 61% of respondents,

27
00:02:15,880 --> 00:02:22,180
global workspace theory by 58, higher order theories by 54, integrated information theory

28
00:02:22,180 --> 00:02:28,680
by 45, and recurrent processing by 39. Quantum theories drew the most votes as not promising.

29
00:02:29,120 --> 00:02:35,260
About two-thirds said machines could be conscious, now, or in the future. It was a self-selected

30
00:02:35,260 --> 00:02:42,100
sample, but it tells you the field has favorites and no consensus. Before the theories, a problem

31
00:02:42,100 --> 00:02:49,200
with the evidence for them. In 2022, Irei Yarin, Lucia Meloni, Michael Pitts, and Lyad Mudrick

32
00:02:49,200 --> 00:02:55,000
built a database of 412 experiments that interpreted their results in light of one

33
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:00,500
or more of the four leading theories. They found that whether a study ended up supporting a given

34
00:03:00,500 --> 00:03:05,920
theory could be predicted from its methodological choices alone, before you looked at the results.

35
00:03:06,140 --> 00:03:11,700
In other words, the kind of experiment a lab chose strongly predicted which theory it would

36
00:03:11,700 --> 00:03:17,120
find support for. They also found that most studies interpreted their findings after the fact,

37
00:03:17,340 --> 00:03:20,940
rather than testing a theory's critical predictions in advance.

38
00:03:21,260 --> 00:03:26,600
So the field has been running the garden of forking paths at the level of whole paradigms.

39
00:03:26,780 --> 00:03:30,280
Each camp picks the experiments its theory is good at.

40
00:03:30,500 --> 00:03:36,460
That's the uncharitable version, and there's truth in it, though nobody needs to be acting in bad faith.

41
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:41,380
Theories grow up around the phenomena that inspired them, and labs specialize.

42
00:03:41,720 --> 00:03:45,300
But piles of confirming studies tell you less than they seem to,

43
00:03:45,560 --> 00:03:51,280
which is the reason for the field's most important development this decade, adversarial collaborations.

44
00:03:51,700 --> 00:03:55,560
The Templeton World Charity Foundation has funded a series of them.

45
00:03:56,280 --> 00:04:00,920
Proponents of rival theories agree in advance on experiments whose outcomes would embarrass

46
00:04:00,920 --> 00:04:04,060
at least one of them, and neutral teams run the experiments.

47
00:04:04,440 --> 00:04:09,880
The program has pitted integrated information theory against global neuronal workspace theory

48
00:04:09,880 --> 00:04:16,100
twice, first-order theories against higher-order ones, and integrated information theory against

49
00:04:16,100 --> 00:04:17,120
predictive processing.

50
00:04:17,420 --> 00:04:23,320
One workshop tried to design a test pitting integrated information theory against ORCH-O-R.

51
00:04:23,320 --> 00:04:25,940
the quantum theory from episode 9,

52
00:04:26,340 --> 00:04:30,520
and concluded that it could not find an experiment suitable for a head-to-head test,

53
00:04:30,720 --> 00:04:32,540
which is informative in itself.

54
00:04:32,900 --> 00:04:35,460
Keep that in mind as we go through the theories.

55
00:04:35,680 --> 00:04:39,640
What each claims, its best evidence, its best counter-evidence,

56
00:04:39,780 --> 00:04:41,460
and what it implies for machines.

57
00:04:42,720 --> 00:04:44,760
First, global workspace theory.

58
00:04:45,080 --> 00:04:48,780
Bernard Bars proposed it in 1988 with a theater metaphor.

59
00:04:49,180 --> 00:04:51,480
Most of what the brain does happens backstage,

60
00:05:03,760 --> 00:05:04,660
Thank you.

61
00:05:23,320 --> 00:05:29,600
cortex. Its signature event is ignition. When a stimulus crosses into awareness, activity does not

62
00:05:29,600 --> 00:05:35,540
just grow. It suddenly amplifies and spreads across the network, all or nothing, typically a few

63
00:05:35,540 --> 00:05:41,400
hundred milliseconds after the stimulus. DeHaan describes four markers, a late brainwave called

64
00:05:41,400 --> 00:05:47,600
the P3B, a sudden nonlinear amplification of activity, bursts of high-frequency oscillation,

65
00:05:47,600 --> 00:05:53,880
and synchronization between distant regions. In 2018, Bram Van Vucket, Piotr Rulfsema,

66
00:05:54,080 --> 00:05:59,460
and colleagues recorded from monkeys detecting faint stimuli. When the monkey reported seeing

67
00:05:59,460 --> 00:06:04,520
the stimulus, activity propagated up to prefrontal cortex and sustained itself there,

68
00:06:04,680 --> 00:06:11,220
all or none. When it missed, the signal was lost along the way. And higher baseline activity in

69
00:06:11,220 --> 00:06:17,080
prefrontal cortex predicted false alarms. The theory explains the limited capacity of awareness,

70
00:06:17,600 --> 00:06:22,380
and why consciously seen information is available for flexible, multi-step reasoning,

71
00:06:22,660 --> 00:06:24,700
while unseen information is not.

72
00:06:24,960 --> 00:06:28,160
It's also the theory most naturally built into machines,

73
00:06:28,460 --> 00:06:31,880
because broadcast through a shared, limited-capacity medium

74
00:06:31,880 --> 00:06:34,060
is something an engineer can design.

75
00:06:34,260 --> 00:06:37,920
The trouble comes from the report Confound of Last Episode.

76
00:06:38,460 --> 00:06:42,380
In Inattentional Blindness Experiments by Michael Pitts and colleagues,

77
00:06:42,760 --> 00:06:46,160
the P3B appeared when stimuli were relevant to the task,

78
00:06:47,600 --> 00:06:51,380
when people saw the same stimuli without needing to report them.

79
00:06:51,540 --> 00:06:55,920
An earlier signal over the back of the brain, at around 200 to 300 milliseconds,

80
00:06:56,380 --> 00:07:00,440
called the visual awareness negativity, tracked awareness more faithfully.

81
00:07:00,700 --> 00:07:05,040
That suggests the classic marker reflects what you do with an experience

82
00:07:05,040 --> 00:07:07,140
rather than the experience itself.

83
00:07:07,720 --> 00:07:11,600
Workspace theorists reply that ignition need not involve report,

84
00:07:11,780 --> 00:07:13,320
and the argument continues.

85
00:07:14,520 --> 00:07:16,500
Second, higher-order theories.

86
00:07:17,600 --> 00:07:22,720
views, a mental state is conscious when you are, in a suitable way, aware of being in it.

87
00:07:23,020 --> 00:07:28,120
The philosopher David Rosenthal developed the higher-order thought version. A perception

88
00:07:28,120 --> 00:07:32,800
becomes conscious when it's accompanied by a thought that you are having it. The classic

89
00:07:32,800 --> 00:07:37,700
objection concerns empty higher-order states. Suppose the higher-order thought says you're

90
00:07:37,700 --> 00:07:42,400
seeing red, but there's no red perception underneath at all. What is it like to be you?

91
00:07:42,940 --> 00:07:47,220
Rosenthal bites the bullet. You'd seem to see red, so you'd be conscious of red.

92
00:07:47,600 --> 00:07:53,020
Critics like Ned Block reply that the theory has now located experience in the higher-order state,

93
00:07:53,340 --> 00:07:54,580
leaving the perception idle.

94
00:07:54,860 --> 00:08:00,080
The most influential current version is Hakwin Lau's perceptual reality monitoring theory.

95
00:08:00,640 --> 00:08:07,860
Sensory systems produce activity all the time, from real inputs, from imagination, from memory, from noise.

96
00:08:08,320 --> 00:08:12,500
Consciousness, on Lau's view, arises when a higher-order mechanism

97
00:08:12,500 --> 00:08:17,040
tags a sensory representation as a reliable reflection of the world,

98
00:08:17,600 --> 00:08:23,520
now. Lau has compared this to a generative adversarial network, in which a discriminator

99
00:08:23,520 --> 00:08:29,180
learns to tell real data from generated data. That gives you a mechanism for hallucination

100
00:08:29,180 --> 00:08:36,320
for free. The discriminator misfires and tags imagination as real. And Lau uses it exactly

101
00:08:36,320 --> 00:08:42,280
that way. Hallucinations as failures of reality monitoring, and blindsight as the opposite

102
00:08:42,280 --> 00:08:48,460
failure, where real signals do not get tagged. The evidence for this family includes experiments

103
00:08:48,460 --> 00:08:54,220
in which people perform equally well on a task but differ in how aware or confident they feel,

104
00:08:54,420 --> 00:08:59,780
with matching differences in prefrontal activity. There's a cautionary tale from this camp, too.

105
00:09:00,180 --> 00:09:05,840
A 2010 study found that magnetic stimulation of prefrontal cortex reduced people's ability to

106
00:09:05,840 --> 00:09:11,140
judge their own accuracy without impairing the task itself, and it was widely cited as support.

107
00:09:12,280 --> 00:09:18,580
2017, Daniel Bohr and colleagues tried to reproduce it with 90 participants, then ran a follow-up and

108
00:09:18,580 --> 00:09:24,960
found no effect. The statistics favored the null. One more classic to downgrade. The main empirical

109
00:09:24,960 --> 00:09:30,600
objection to higher-order theories is that large prefrontal lesions do not seem to abolish

110
00:09:30,600 --> 00:09:37,400
consciousness. In 2017, the Journal of Neuroscience published a paired debate on precisely this.

111
00:09:37,400 --> 00:09:41,740
One team, including Melanie Boley, Koch, and Giulio Tononi,

112
00:09:42,220 --> 00:09:46,240
argued that the neural correlates of consciousness lie mainly in the back of the brain.

113
00:09:46,520 --> 00:09:49,840
The other, Brian Odegaard, Robert Knight, and Lau,

114
00:09:50,320 --> 00:09:52,500
argued that prefrontal cortex is essential

115
00:09:52,500 --> 00:09:55,440
and that the negative findings were weaker than claimed.

116
00:09:55,780 --> 00:09:59,500
That front-versus-back fight runs through the rest of this episode.

117
00:10:00,700 --> 00:10:04,000
Third, recurrent processing theory from Victor Lama.

118
00:10:04,490 --> 00:10:11,400
When a visual stimulus arrives, a fast feed-forward sweep carries it up the visual hierarchy in about a tenth of a second,

119
00:10:11,680 --> 00:10:15,500
enough to drive quick responses, but on this view unconscious.

120
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:21,420
Then feedback connections kick in, creating local recurrent processing within sensory cortex.

121
00:10:22,240 --> 00:10:26,520
Lama argues that this local recurrence is sufficient for phenomenal experience.

122
00:10:26,820 --> 00:10:32,760
Wider recurrence involving frontal areas adds access, report, memory, and control.

123
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:38,940
includes the timing of masking, which seems to work by interrupting recurrent processing,

124
00:10:39,240 --> 00:10:45,220
and experiments in which a magnetic pulse over primary visual cortex, delivered roughly a tenth

125
00:10:45,220 --> 00:10:50,700
of a second after a stimulus, when feedback should be arriving, can abolish awareness of it.

126
00:10:50,900 --> 00:10:56,060
Recurrent processing theory is the natural ally of Bloch's overflow view from episode 7,

127
00:10:56,640 --> 00:11:02,660
experience in local loops, access for only some of it. Global workspace theory sits on the other

128
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:05,320
No success, no experience.

129
00:11:05,760 --> 00:11:09,160
Its main vulnerability is the refrigerator light problem.

130
00:11:09,480 --> 00:11:13,540
If local recurrence produces experiences that never reach report,

131
00:11:13,840 --> 00:11:15,840
how will we ever confirm they exist?

132
00:11:16,140 --> 00:11:19,160
For machines, the theory has a clean implication.

133
00:11:19,740 --> 00:11:22,460
Purely feed-forward computation is not enough.

134
00:11:22,820 --> 00:11:23,900
Recurrence matters.

135
00:11:25,160 --> 00:11:30,100
Fourth, integrated information theory, whose philosophy we met in episode 9.

136
00:11:30,540 --> 00:11:31,660
Here is the science.

137
00:11:32,320 --> 00:11:38,040
IIT predicts that the physical substrate of experience lies mainly in a posterior hot zone

138
00:11:38,040 --> 00:11:44,160
of the cortex, rather than in prefrontal cortex. The dreaming studies from last episode fit that

139
00:11:44,160 --> 00:11:50,460
prediction, and the Zappan-Zipp complexity measure was inspired by IIT's idea that consciousness

140
00:11:50,460 --> 00:11:56,340
requires both integration and differentiation. It also explains a striking fact about the

141
00:11:56,340 --> 00:12:02,960
cerebellum, which contains about 69 billion of the brain's 86 billion neurons, four out of five.

142
00:12:03,380 --> 00:12:09,360
Yet damage to it, even its complete absence in rare people born without one, affects movement,

143
00:12:09,680 --> 00:12:16,160
speech, and sometimes learning, while consciousness remains. On IIT's account, the cerebellum's highly

144
00:12:16,160 --> 00:12:22,700
modular, parallel architecture generates little integrated information, despite its huge neuron

145
00:12:22,700 --> 00:12:30,360
count. The skeptics of episode 13 lean on exactly this example. IIT makes stranger predictions too.

146
00:12:30,640 --> 00:12:36,000
Because its measure, phi, depends on what a system could do, not only on what it's doing,

147
00:12:36,500 --> 00:12:41,340
IIT predicts that neurons which are inactive but could fire still contribute to experience,

148
00:12:41,720 --> 00:12:47,320
while the same neurons silenced pharmacologically would not. That's testable in principle,

149
00:12:47,320 --> 00:12:49,780
though nobody has yet done it convincingly.

150
00:12:50,040 --> 00:12:52,360
Now the criticisms, which are severe.

151
00:12:52,660 --> 00:12:56,760
The cost of computing phi exactly explodes with the number of elements,

152
00:12:56,940 --> 00:12:59,520
so it has only been computed for tiny systems,

153
00:12:59,760 --> 00:13:04,120
using software called PyPhi developed by Will Maynor and colleagues.

154
00:13:04,600 --> 00:13:08,080
In practice, every empirical test uses proxies.

155
00:13:08,400 --> 00:13:11,800
Then there's the unfolding argument from Adrian Doerig,

156
00:13:12,080 --> 00:13:15,780
Aaron Scherger, Michael Herzog, and colleagues in 2019.

157
00:13:17,320 --> 00:13:23,240
network can be unfolded into a purely feedforward network that computes exactly the same input-output

158
00:13:23,240 --> 00:13:29,500
function. IIT assigns substantial phi to the recurrent version and zero to the feedforward

159
00:13:29,500 --> 00:13:34,640
one, but every experiment we can run is read through outputs, so it would treat the two

160
00:13:34,640 --> 00:13:41,140
identically. Either IIT's claims about which system is conscious can never be tested, or they're false.

161
00:13:41,300 --> 00:13:46,260
And the same argument, its authors point out, threatens recurrent processing theory.

162
00:13:47,320 --> 00:13:51,040
Devastating if you think consciousness must make a functional difference.

163
00:13:51,260 --> 00:13:57,700
Two behaviorally identical systems, and the theory says one is conscious and the other is not.

164
00:13:57,900 --> 00:14:01,000
Which is exactly where IIT's defenders dig in.

165
00:14:01,720 --> 00:14:05,440
Consciousness, they argue, is about intrinsic causal structure,

166
00:14:05,640 --> 00:14:11,940
which perturbation experiments like ZAP and ZIP can probe directly beyond input-output behavior.

167
00:14:12,430 --> 00:14:16,000
And they argue that the critics are smuggling in a functionalist assumption.

168
00:14:17,320 --> 00:14:23,400
reply depends on your answer to episode 7. Then there's the logic gate grid from episode 9,

169
00:14:23,720 --> 00:14:29,980
which Tononi accepted rather than retreat. In September 2023, the tension boiled over.

170
00:14:31,240 --> 00:14:37,980
124 researchers signed an open letter calling IIT pseudoscience, arguing that its core claims

171
00:14:37,980 --> 00:14:43,340
are untestable and that media coverage had overstated its support. Many researchers,

172
00:14:43,340 --> 00:14:50,480
including some who reject IIT, thought the label went much too far. In 2025, the fight moved into

173
00:14:50,480 --> 00:14:57,220
nature neuroscience, an expanded critique, a reply from Tone Oni and 21 co-authors arguing that the

174
00:14:57,220 --> 00:15:03,020
label reflected a bias toward computational functionalism, and a third piece by Alex Gomez

175
00:15:03,020 --> 00:15:08,960
Marin and Anil Seth calling for the field to get past the name calling. The underlying question

176
00:15:08,960 --> 00:15:14,540
is a real one. Can a theory of consciousness be both scientific and non-functionalist?

177
00:15:15,700 --> 00:15:21,420
Fifth, the predictive processing family, which grows out of the predictive brain of episode 5.

178
00:15:22,140 --> 00:15:26,820
Anil Seth's version is the beast machine. If perception is the brain's best guess about the

179
00:15:26,820 --> 00:15:31,420
causes of its sensory signals, a controlled hallucination, then the deepest layer of

180
00:15:31,420 --> 00:15:37,380
prediction concerns the body itself. Heartbeat, blood pressure, gut, temperature, the signals

181
00:15:38,960 --> 00:15:42,280
The brain predicts and regulates these to keep the organism alive.

182
00:15:42,720 --> 00:15:47,420
Seth argues that emotions, moods, and the most basic feeling of being a self

183
00:15:47,420 --> 00:15:49,300
are rooted in that predictive regulation.

184
00:15:49,580 --> 00:15:54,240
We're conscious, on this view, not despite being animals, but because we are animals.

185
00:15:54,520 --> 00:15:58,060
There's suggestive evidence that bodily signals shape perception.

186
00:15:58,340 --> 00:16:02,060
In 2014, Hyondang Park and colleagues in Paris

187
00:16:02,060 --> 00:16:04,600
found that the brain's response to each heartbeat

188
00:16:04,600 --> 00:16:07,960
predicted whether people would detect a faint visual stimulus.

189
00:16:08,960 --> 00:16:14,300
Lisa Feldman Barrett's theory of constructed emotion makes a related claim. Emotions are

190
00:16:14,300 --> 00:16:20,200
predictions grounded in interosption. Mark Solms pushes further down. In The Hidden Spring,

191
00:16:20,280 --> 00:16:25,960
and in a 2019 paper on the hard problem and the free energy principle, he argues that consciousness

192
00:16:25,960 --> 00:16:32,160
originates not in the cortex but in the upper brainstem. It begins, he says, as raw feeling

193
00:16:32,160 --> 00:16:38,720
effect, which registers how well the organism is doing at staying alive. Rising uncertainty felt

194
00:16:38,960 --> 00:16:44,880
unpleasant, its resolution as pleasant. He draws on Jock Panksepp's work on emotional circuits

195
00:16:44,880 --> 00:16:50,060
deep in the brain, and on Bjorn Merker's observations of children born with little or

196
00:16:50,060 --> 00:16:56,220
no cerebral cortex, who nonetheless seem alert, emotionally responsive, and able to recognize

197
00:16:56,220 --> 00:17:02,140
the people they know. Merker's target article in Behavioral and Brain Sciences drew commentaries

198
00:17:02,140 --> 00:17:07,900
showing how contested this is. Some of these children retain islands of cortex, and critics

199
00:17:08,960 --> 00:17:15,780
fall short of establishing experience. Antonio Damasio, from Descartes' error onward, has long

200
00:17:15,780 --> 00:17:21,780
argued that feeling is grounded in the body's homeostasis. On the other side, Joseph Ledoux

201
00:17:21,780 --> 00:17:26,660
and Richard Brown argue that emotional experiences are higher-order cortical states,

202
00:17:26,880 --> 00:17:33,020
and that the deep survival circuits produce defensive responses, not felt fear. Which side

203
00:17:33,020 --> 00:17:38,120
is right decides what we should say about fish, newborns, and patients with little cortex?

204
00:17:38,960 --> 00:17:45,120
in 2025, Ruben Lachonen, Carl Friston, and Shamal Shandaria proposed an active inference

205
00:17:45,120 --> 00:17:51,480
theory called a beautiful loop. It names three conditions. A unified, simulated model of the

206
00:17:51,480 --> 00:17:56,920
world, which they call the epistemic field, a competition among inferences, in which those

207
00:17:56,920 --> 00:18:02,880
that best reduce long-term uncertainty win and are bound together. An epistemic depth,

208
00:18:03,120 --> 00:18:06,940
in which the model's beliefs are shared recurrently through its own hierarchy,

209
00:18:06,940 --> 00:18:10,300
so that the model contains knowledge of its own existence.

210
00:18:10,640 --> 00:18:11,680
That's the loop.

211
00:18:11,900 --> 00:18:16,180
The authors use the meditative states from last episode as test cases.

212
00:18:16,520 --> 00:18:18,600
What does this family imply for machines?

213
00:18:18,860 --> 00:18:20,760
It depends which member you ask.

214
00:18:21,160 --> 00:18:24,740
On Seth's beast machine view, prediction alone is not enough.

215
00:18:25,120 --> 00:18:27,980
Prediction in the service of staying alive is what matters,

216
00:18:28,220 --> 00:18:31,180
so today's disembodied systems are poor candidates.

217
00:18:31,800 --> 00:18:34,880
On a more purely computational reading of active inference,

218
00:18:34,880 --> 00:18:39,600
an artificial agent with the right self-modeling architecture could qualify.

219
00:18:40,880 --> 00:18:43,900
Sixth, Michael Graziano's attention schema theory,

220
00:18:44,200 --> 00:18:46,560
the illusionist's mechanism from episode 8.

221
00:18:46,800 --> 00:18:49,860
As a scientific theory, it makes a specific prediction.

222
00:18:50,280 --> 00:18:52,660
Awareness should matter for controlling attention.

223
00:18:52,920 --> 00:18:58,760
And that's roughly what experiments by Taylor Webb, Graziano, and colleagues found in 2016.

224
00:18:59,360 --> 00:19:02,240
Q's people were not aware of still captured their attention.

225
00:19:02,740 --> 00:19:07,600
But without awareness, people controlled their attention less well, holding it less stably.

226
00:19:08,500 --> 00:19:13,040
Transformers are literally built out of attention. Do they have attention schemas?

227
00:19:13,380 --> 00:19:15,540
Not in Graziano's sense.

228
00:19:15,960 --> 00:19:18,840
Attention in a transformer is a routing mechanism.

229
00:19:19,260 --> 00:19:21,940
Each position weighs information from the others.

230
00:19:22,200 --> 00:19:25,960
It does not come with a model of that attention used to control it.

231
00:19:26,300 --> 00:19:30,020
Whether something like that could be learned implicitly is an open question,

232
00:19:30,020 --> 00:19:34,800
and it's one of the indicators researchers check for, as we'll see next episode.

233
00:19:35,900 --> 00:19:41,620
Seventh, unlimited associative learning from Simona Ginsberg and Eva Yablonka.

234
00:19:41,900 --> 00:19:45,680
Rather than a mechanism, it identifies a transition marker,

235
00:19:45,920 --> 00:19:50,300
an evolved capacity whose presence indicates that consciousness has arisen.

236
00:19:50,540 --> 00:19:56,540
Unlimited associative learning is open-ended learning about novel, compound stimuli and actions,

237
00:19:56,540 --> 00:20:00,520
combinations of features across time gaps in chains.

238
00:20:00,940 --> 00:20:04,560
On their account, it first evolved around the Cambrian explosion

239
00:20:04,560 --> 00:20:06,940
more than 500 million years ago,

240
00:20:07,080 --> 00:20:09,680
and consciousness evolved at least three times

241
00:20:09,680 --> 00:20:13,080
in vertebrates, in arthropods, and in cephalopods.

242
00:20:13,440 --> 00:20:16,860
Language models learn associations open-endedly.

243
00:20:17,120 --> 00:20:18,800
By that marker, they are in.

244
00:20:19,340 --> 00:20:21,580
Ginzberg and Yablonka would say no.

245
00:20:21,820 --> 00:20:25,020
In a 2021 paper with Zohar Bronfman on

246
00:20:26,540 --> 00:20:30,520
they argued that a machine would need this kind of learning tied to a body

247
00:20:30,520 --> 00:20:33,920
and to one overriding value, keeping itself going.

248
00:20:34,360 --> 00:20:36,100
Language models have neither.

249
00:20:36,280 --> 00:20:40,940
And that shows the general problem with transplanting any marker calibrated on animals.

250
00:20:41,920 --> 00:20:44,320
A few other theories deserve a mention.

251
00:20:44,820 --> 00:20:50,040
Dendritic integration theory, from Yan Aru, Matotaka Suzuki, and Matthew Larkham,

252
00:20:50,480 --> 00:20:54,460
ties consciousness to a cellular mechanism in large cortical neurons,

253
00:20:54,460 --> 00:20:58,700
and in rodents, anesthetics appear to work in part by disrupting it.

254
00:20:59,440 --> 00:21:05,200
Serial Penartz's neurorepresentationalism treats consciousness as the brain's multimodal,

255
00:21:05,480 --> 00:21:11,080
situational model of the world. And electromagnetic field theories, like John Joe McFadden's,

256
00:21:11,240 --> 00:21:15,400
locate the integration of experience in the brain's own electric field.

257
00:21:15,660 --> 00:21:22,300
The skeptics of episode 13 cite recent work from Earl Miller's lab suggesting that the brain's

258
00:21:22,300 --> 00:21:28,180
electric fields help coordinate activity across the cortex, as one-way biology might matter.

259
00:21:29,060 --> 00:21:35,840
Now the trials. The first, and so far the most important, was the Cogitate Consortium's test

260
00:21:35,840 --> 00:21:42,760
of global neuronal workspace theory against integrated information theory. 256 participants

261
00:21:42,760 --> 00:21:51,020
were tested with three methods, fMRIM, e.g., and electrodes implanted in the brains of 34 epilepsy

262
00:21:52,300 --> 00:21:58,440
They saw faces, objects, letters, and meaningless symbols at different orientations and durations,

263
00:21:58,740 --> 00:22:01,780
sometimes relevant to their task and sometimes not.

264
00:22:02,060 --> 00:22:04,240
The theories made opposing predictions.

265
00:22:05,040 --> 00:22:08,980
IIT predicted that conscious content would be maintained in the back of the brain

266
00:22:08,980 --> 00:22:14,700
for as long as the stimulus was seen, with sustained synchronization between posterior regions.

267
00:22:15,100 --> 00:22:19,040
Global workspace theory predicted ignition in prefrontal cortex

268
00:22:19,040 --> 00:22:22,120
when the stimulus appeared and again when it disappeared.

269
00:22:22,300 --> 00:22:25,960
and that prefrontal cortex would carry the details of what was seen.

270
00:22:26,200 --> 00:22:30,820
The results, published in Nature in April 2025, challenged both.

271
00:22:31,180 --> 00:22:32,680
Some predictions held up.

272
00:22:32,820 --> 00:22:36,240
Some sites at the back tracked how long a stimulus lasted

273
00:22:36,240 --> 00:22:39,080
and prefrontal cortex responded when it appeared.

274
00:22:39,260 --> 00:22:40,940
But key predictions failed.

275
00:22:41,160 --> 00:22:45,880
There was no sustained, content-specific synchronization within posterior cortex,

276
00:22:46,200 --> 00:22:49,380
which the authors called the most direct challenge to IIT.

277
00:22:49,380 --> 00:22:52,380
and there was no ignition when the stimulus disappeared.

278
00:22:52,940 --> 00:22:58,520
Prefrontal cortex carried information about the category of what was seen, face versus object,

279
00:22:58,900 --> 00:23:03,360
but not about its exact identity and little or nothing about its orientation.

280
00:23:03,780 --> 00:23:07,060
That's a serious problem for global workspace theory.

281
00:23:07,380 --> 00:23:10,440
So the first decisive test was indecisive.

282
00:23:10,920 --> 00:23:14,480
Decisive about some predictions, indecisive about the theories,

283
00:23:14,740 --> 00:23:16,860
which is how science usually works.

284
00:23:17,440 --> 00:23:20,000
Both camps disputed parts of the interpretation,

285
00:23:20,320 --> 00:23:24,600
and the pseudoscience letter erupted a few months after the first results were presented.

286
00:23:24,980 --> 00:23:27,240
But the method is the real result.

287
00:23:27,700 --> 00:23:32,700
Theories made risky, pre-registered predictions, and some of those predictions failed.

288
00:23:32,980 --> 00:23:34,500
More rounds are underway.

289
00:23:35,260 --> 00:23:37,720
Cogitate's second experiment uses a video game.

290
00:23:38,080 --> 00:23:41,340
People play while faces and objects appear in the background,

291
00:23:41,340 --> 00:23:43,620
so their attention is pulled away from them,

292
00:23:43,620 --> 00:23:47,260
and later watch replays of gameplay while attending to those images.

293
00:23:47,660 --> 00:23:52,020
It was introduced in posters at the field's 2025 meeting in Crete,

294
00:23:52,220 --> 00:23:57,420
with 139 participants across FMR, I&M, EG.

295
00:23:57,720 --> 00:24:01,460
Those posters showed that the game works and gave early mixed results,

296
00:24:01,740 --> 00:24:03,980
weak support for a key workspace prediction,

297
00:24:04,240 --> 00:24:06,760
and only partial support for one of IITs.

298
00:24:07,160 --> 00:24:11,340
None of it is peer-reviewed yet, and no verdict on the theories has been published.

299
00:24:11,340 --> 00:24:19,200
A separate collaboration, called Intrepid, is testing IIT against active inference and against neurorepresentationalism,

300
00:24:19,340 --> 00:24:22,600
with four experiments and independent replications planned.

301
00:24:22,960 --> 00:24:24,660
Its results are pending, too.

302
00:24:25,940 --> 00:24:27,060
What would settle things?

303
00:24:27,240 --> 00:24:28,720
A few candidate tests.

304
00:24:29,120 --> 00:24:32,460
IIT's inactive neuron prediction, if anyone can make it work.

305
00:24:32,800 --> 00:24:39,660
Lesion and stimulation studies of prefrontal cortex that are large enough and pre-registered to settle the front-versus-back fight.

306
00:24:41,340 --> 00:24:43,600
that cleanly separate experience from access,

307
00:24:43,840 --> 00:24:46,260
and the extreme states of last episode,

308
00:24:46,480 --> 00:24:48,480
cessations, ketamine dreams,

309
00:24:48,880 --> 00:24:50,200
awareness without content,

310
00:24:50,480 --> 00:24:52,420
which any complete theory has to handle.

311
00:24:52,780 --> 00:24:54,980
Meanwhile, let me line the theories up

312
00:24:54,980 --> 00:24:57,700
against the question this series is building toward.

313
00:24:58,140 --> 00:25:01,380
Global workspace theory says a machine could be conscious

314
00:25:01,380 --> 00:25:03,860
if it has a limited capacity workspace

315
00:25:03,860 --> 00:25:07,580
whose contents are broadcast to many specialized processes.

316
00:25:08,240 --> 00:25:10,100
Higher order theories say yes

317
00:25:11,340 --> 00:25:17,520
its own representations in the right way. Recurrent processing theory requires genuine recurrence.

318
00:25:18,220 --> 00:25:23,720
Attention schema theory requires a model of the machine's own attention, used to control it.

319
00:25:24,340 --> 00:25:29,360
Integrated information theory says no for conventional digital hardware, whatever the

320
00:25:29,360 --> 00:25:34,980
software. The beast machine version of predictive processing makes systems that are not regulating

321
00:25:34,980 --> 00:25:41,340
a living body very unlikely candidates. And unlimited associative learning, on its author's

322
00:25:41,340 --> 00:25:46,980
account, would require open-ended learning in a body, driven by something like self-preservation,

323
00:25:47,320 --> 00:25:52,620
which today's systems lack. So whether today's AI systems are candidates for consciousness

324
00:25:52,620 --> 00:25:58,300
depends almost entirely on which theory you believe, and no theory has won. That is not a

325
00:25:58,300 --> 00:26:03,380
reason for despair. It's the starting point for the most careful attempts yet to assess machines.

326
00:26:05,000 --> 00:26:11,220
a group of 19 researchers turned most of these theories, though not IIT, into a checklist of

327
00:26:11,220 --> 00:26:16,400
architectural indicators that you can look for in an AI system. That's where Part 3 begins.

